# Volute — the assets you produce

Volute ships with no pre-made art and no pre-made sound. This machine carries
asset tools on its `PATH`, and every sprite, sheet, particle system, and cue the
game plays is produced with them, committed, and wired in. This file is the
contract: what to produce, which tool produces it, where the file lands, and
how the build reaches it.

Production is a one-time step. The tools belong to this machine and are absent
when the project is installed and rebuilt elsewhere, so the committed files are
the assets and the build bundles them. `npm ci` and `npm run build` invoke no
tool, and the built site fetches nothing from outside its own `dist/`.

## The tools

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | one sprite, a PNG | the cores, the machinery marks, the injector, the intake maw, the channel plate, the HUD icons |
| `draw-sheet` | a sprite sheet, one PNG per frame | the fire recoil, the maw swallow, the extraction flash |
| `particle-2d` | a particle system, a `system.json` | the extraction burst, the bore detonation, the intake swallow spray, the pickup shimmer |
| `sfx-synth` | a procedural sound, a `.wav` | the injector's cues and the pickup |
| `sfx-sample` | a sampled sound over the sample pack, a `.wav` | the extraction steps, the intake, and the run's cues |
| `music` | sequenced music over the instrument bank, a `.wav` and a `.mid` | the two music beds |

Each is a command-line tool, and `<tool> --help` states its operations
(`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags). In outline, each records
the operations run against it and then renders the finished file. `draw` and
`draw-sheet` rasterize a fixed-size RGBA canvas from drawing operations;
`draw-sheet` takes a `--frame` on every operation and lands a sheet as separate
files, one PNG per frame. `particle-2d` authors emitters, forces, and
per-particle curves into a system that is simulated live rather than baked into
frames. The audio tools record synth voices, sampled layers, or sequenced notes
and render a PCM `.wav`; `sfx-sample` draws on the `combat-core` sample pack and
`music` on the `synthwave` instrument bank, both present in the container and
browsed with `list-samples` and `list-instruments`, and `music` emits a portable
`.mid` beside the `.wav` that plays.

## The hall

The mood is a working geothermal pump hall, deep and hot: cast metal, plate worn
by decades of running, condensation, and mineral that carries its own light. The
cores are the one bright thing in it, and the intake is the one threatening
thing. Palette, type, and styling are yours, chosen once and held across every
sprite, effect, and screen so the hall reads as one place.

## Where the files land, and how they are drawn

Every produced file loads page-relative, since the built site is served from a
sub-path as well as from a root, and the project's Vite config already emits
relative URLs.

The picture is layered the same way whatever draws it: the channel plate under
the train, the cores with their marks and the injector above it, the sheets and
the particle effects above those, and the HUD and the screens last.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Commit the produced files under `assets/`, and resolve each PNG, `system.json`,
and `.wav` through the bundler. The build owns the loading, the decoding, and the
2D context: every image is decoded, every system parsed, and every cue ready
before the first frame that draws or plays it, and a `reset` of the game's state
leaves every one of them loaded.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Commit the produced files under `public/assets/`, which the engine serves at its
asset root and the build copies into `dist/` unchanged. Load every image with
`api.assets.loadImage` and every cue with `api.audio.load`, awaited in
`initialize`, so nothing is still decoding on the first frame. `render` draws the
loaded images into the frame's context in that order.
{{else}}
Commit the produced files under `public/assets/`, which the engine serves at its
asset root and the build copies into `dist/` unchanged. Load them through the
engine's asset loaders before the world that needs them is built. Each core,
projectile, injector, and intake actor carries its produced sprite on a sprite
component, and the channel plate, the sheets, and the effects take layers that
hold that order.
{{/if}}

## The sprites

Produce each of these as a single PNG with `draw`, on a transparent canvas. Every
sprite is pixel art drawn at one unit per pixel, so a sprite 28 pixels wide
stands 28 units wide on the field, and the game samples it nearest-neighbor so it
stays crisp at the canvas fit.

| Sprite | Canvas | Count |
| --- | --- | --- |
| core, one per charge | 28 x 28 | 5 |
| machinery mark | 16 x 16 | 4 |
| injector base | 44 x 44 | 1 |
| injector barrel | 44 x 20 | 1 |
| intake maw | 64 x 64 | 1 |
| channel plate | 64 x 64 | 1 |
| HUD cell icon | 24 x 24 | 1 |
| pressure icon | 24 x 24 | 1 |

The five cores fill their canvas as round, faceted mineral, each carrying the
color and the glyph its charge is drawn with, and each reads clearly against the
channel plate and against the field. A marked core draws its mark over its core
sprite, and the four marks, `choke`, `backflow`, `bore`, and `sightline`, are
told apart from each other at that size.

The injector base and barrel read as one machine seated on the field. Author the
barrel pointing toward `+x` so the game rotates it to the aim, and give its
pointing end a silhouette unmistakable at any angle. The intake maw reads as an
opening that takes cores in and is the heaviest object on the field. The channel
plate is a tile that sits flush against itself on all four sides, quiet enough
that a core standing on it is the brighter of the two. The two HUD icons read at
24 units against the HUD's own ground.

## The sheets

Produce each of these with `draw-sheet`, one PNG per frame, and animate the
frames in the game.

| Sheet | Frames | Canvas | Plays |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| injector fire recoil | 4 | 44 x 20 | once per shot, over the barrel |
| intake maw swallow | 6 | 64 x 64 | once per core the intake takes, over the maw |
| extraction flash | 6 | 48 x 48 | once over each core an extraction removes |

The recoil kicks the barrel back along its own axis and returns it, and it plays
through inside the fire cooldown so consecutive shots each read as a shot. The
swallow opens the maw and closes it on the core it takes. The extraction flash is
shared by every charge, tinted in the game to the charge that extracted, and it
plays through before the recoil hold ends, so a run of extractions reads as a run
rather than as one long glare.

## The effects

Produce each of these with `particle-2d` as a `system.json`, and play it live.

| System | Fired at | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| extraction burst | each core an extraction removes | a sharp outward throw in the extracted charge's color, gone almost as fast as it arrives |
| bore detonation | the extraction point of a bore | heavier and slower than the burst, reading across the whole ground the bore clears |
| intake swallow spray | the intake, as it takes a core | a short spray drawn inward and down, reading as something lost rather than won |
| pickup shimmer | the marked core an extraction removes | a bright, brief rise that separates a grant from an ordinary extraction |

Play them through `@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`, an installed dependency
imported by its bare name, using its `./canvas` binding: a player is constructed
over a parsed system and a 2D rendering context and advanced each frame with that
frame's delta, and it simulates the system and composites the particles itself.
Its options carry the seed, the composite mode, and whether the context is
cleared before compositing, and the package's own types are the authoritative
API. The effects sit over the finished frame, and each instance plays at the
position of the event that raised it.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build hands the player the same context it draws the field into.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The context is the one `render` receives, so a live effect advances on the frame
delta `update` was handed.
{{else}}
A draw component is what puts a player's output on the field, so an effect takes
its place in the layer order like any other picture.
{{/if}}

Each play of a system varies, and that variation is correct.

## The sound

Produce a distinct sound for every cue, under exactly these names, and play each
on the event `specs/ui.md` binds it to.

| Cue | Tool | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fire` | `sfx-synth` | a short dry release of pressure, light enough to sound many times a second |
| `seat` | `sfx-synth` | a soft mechanical seat, the sound of a core settling into the train |
| `swap` | `sfx-synth` | a lighter flick, never mistaken by ear for `seat` |
| `denied` | `sfx-synth` | a flat, blunt refusal, unmistakably negative |
| `machinery` | `sfx-synth` | a bright rising acknowledgement that carries over whatever else is sounding |
| `extract-1` to `extract-5` | `sfx-sample` | one sound at five rising steps, each step recognizably the same sound higher, so a chain is heard climbing |
| `intake` | `sfx-sample` | a low rumble with real weight, heard through the music bed |
| `level-clear` | `sfx-sample` | settled and resolved, the sound of a hall run clean |
| `cell-lost` | `sfx-sample` | heavy and final, the loudest bad news the game gives |
| `hall-loop` | `music` | a slow, low, mechanical bed that stays welcome across a long session |
| `danger-loop` | `music` | the same hall under strain, tighter and more insistent, and told from `hall-loop` within a second of starting |

The two music beds are each authored to loop cleanly, the file's end running into
its start with no click, no gap, and no jump in level.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Decode each `.wav` with the Web Audio API, play a cue on its event, and run the
two beds through looping sources.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Bind each name in `CUES` and `EXTRACT_CUES` to its file with `api.audio.load`,
and run the two beds through `api.audio.loop` and `api.audio.stop`.
{{else}}
Bind each name in `CUES` and `EXTRACT_CUES` to its file through the engine's
`audio.load`, and run the two beds through the world's `audio.loop` and
`audio.stop`.
{{/if}}

## What is drawn in code

Everything else on screen is drawn in code, with no produced file behind it: the
HUD's readouts, gauges, and frames, every screen and all of its copy, the debug
overlay, the sightline ray, the danger read, and the routing that lays the
produced plate along the channel and places every sprite on it.

## Genuinely produced

Every core, mark, injector, maw, and icon on screen is a produced sprite; the
barrel, the maw, and each extraction move on produced frames; every burst is a
simulated particle system; and every cue and both beds are produced sound.
Everything the game shows and plays traces to a file produced here or to the
chrome drawn in code above.
